# General Debate on the First Supplementary Estimates for 1968–69
Tamil title: 1968-69ஆம் ஆண்டிற்கான முதலாவது துணை நிதி நிலை அறிக்கைமீது பொது விவாதம்

## Image 6

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** The hon. Member is perfectly right. If I criticise any Government, it is up to the hon. Member to pull me up but I am making merely a statement of fact.

I therefore wish to state that I cannot accept the suggestion made by the honourable member.

They asked whether producing the newsreel was necessary. It is being produced only because the Government considers it necessary. They understand that there can be no doubt about its necessity to the Government, although they themselves may feel that it is unnecessary. We consider it necessary. If only ministers' faces appear in it and other faces do not, we are acting precisely so that every face may appear. If some faces do not appear, we are not responsible for that.

Certain general observations were made on the supplementary demands for grants. I will not hesitate to accord those observations the respect due to them. But I believe you will not suppose that, in according them respect, I must accept every one of them exactly as stated. While speaking, Thiru Ponnappa Nadar said that someone wearing a police uniform had come and beaten people. He did not, however, say that police uniforms had been issued to particular persons. Fortunately, he stopped with the policemen. I am pleased that his imagination remained within that limit and did not extend to saying that none of the existing Deputy Commissioners and Inspectors was genuine and that somebody else had come wearing their uniforms. As one who knows the true facts and bears responsibility for administering the Government, I state that I wholly and completely deny all this. They said that six hundred people had been injured.

## Image 7

Until yesterday, they said that five hundred people had been injured. The newspapers reported three hundred. As far as I am concerned, I would be distressed even if only three people had been injured. I respectfully ask only that they should not suppose they can strengthen the accusation by increasing the number of injured people day after day as they speak. They themselves said that a peaceful procession had been attacked. This has repeatedly been emphasised already, and I wish to emphasise once again now: when permission is granted for a procession, a regulatory order specifies the routes along which it must proceed. The police attacked no one who went along that route, nor did a situation requiring an attack arise.

After the greater part of the procession had proceeded along the authorised route, those in its rear attempted to go in another direction along an unauthorised route. They were dispersed at that point. The information I received is that a lathi charge occurred while they were being dispersed. That is also the information reported in the newspapers. The front of the procession had nearly reached Kannappar Thidal. Those in the rear were dispersed because they attempted to take a different, unauthorised route. If the police had attacked them merely for the sake of attacking them, I would condemn it. Conducting a procession is one of the methods of democracy. But when people leave the allotted route and proceed along another, the police, being responsible for maintaining the law, may have to stop and disperse them. Members alleged that a severe lathi charge took place. I immediately asked the police officer and obtained all the particulars. Do not imagine that what I am about to say is a justification of what happened. We too have been beaten with lathis.

## Image 8

There was no resolution of sympathy or adjournment motion for us. Our comrades were attacked so severely that, because even the Madras hospital had no room to admit them, they had to be cared for in hospitals around Madras. Therefore, if it is thought that we are doing this for that reason—and they said that I have a tender heart, for which I thank them—I must state that this is not so. I say this not as a justification of what happened. But I cannot refrain from pointing out that the matter is being magnified with a political motive and for political propaganda.

**Thiru K. Vinayakam:** While our Chief Minister was describing the lathi charge during this procession, he said, “We did it because they changed direction.” Did you beat them at the place where they changed direction, or did you pursue and beat them all the way to Moore Market? Likewise, why were those who went by way of Chintadripet beaten? The Commissioner of Police issues one statement in the morning and another in the evening. He says that they were beaten only once. Were six hundred people beaten in a single stroke, or were they beaten continuously? There is a conflict in what he says. You ask us, “Did you see all this?” Did you see all this? Do not place excessive trust in the police officers. If you decide that what your officers say must be fair merely because they say it, I wish to tell you that the fate that befell us will one day befall you as well.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** I said so earlier. I did not say that he should suffer on that account. You need not come behind us—you already gave us beforehand all that was due to us.

## Image 9

Please do not imagine that this happened as an act of repayment for what you gave us. That is not so. They said that the report supplied by the police officer contradicts itself. But what you say itself contradicts what you said before. First you said three hundred people, then five hundred, and now it has become six hundred. Why?

**Thiru K. Vinayakam:** Appoint a judicial inquiry.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** A judicial inquiry is unnecessary. They asked why people were beaten from that place all the way to Moore Market. If they had turned back as soon as they were stopped and the barrier was placed, there would have been no need for action. A scuffle took place between them and the police, and it continued. Consequently, force had to be used until they left the place. The fact that two senior police officers and seventy police personnel were also injured in this attack is evidence that there was a counterattack.

Even then, our Ponnappa Nadar will say that policemen wearing some counterfeit uniform attacked policemen wearing a genuine one, and so forth. If he wrote that as a story and filmed it—not for the newsreel, but as a motion picture—he might perhaps receive the one-lakh-rupee prize for imaginative power. I wish to state that this Government will not allow even the slightest room for events to occur in the manner he describes.

Our friend Balasubramaniyan of the Communist Party made a general observation while speaking on this same issue. He said: “This is not merely a matter of one particular incident. In general, the attitude of officials has not changed; police methods too have not changed, and a change must be brought about.” It is a general observation and a general problem. But if we ask whether lathi charges and firing have ceased in places governed by a Communist Party that, like him, calls for fundamental change—

## Image 10

—they have not. I believe they too regret this; I regret it as well. The good change that he and I together look forward to cannot come very simply, very easily, or very quickly. I wish to state that, as far as possible, we are making efforts under this administration to change that attitude.

**Thiru A. Balasubramaniyan:** I made this very point during the previous Budget session. I did not say it applied only under this Government. I specifically said that the same condition exists in Kerala. I have not altered my language to suit the place by saying one thing where one party governs and another where a different party governs. I stated the facts as they occur, and I will always do so.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** That is precisely what I too said. I mentioned it only to show that there is a difference between what our Congress friends said and the standpoint adopted by our Communist Party friends. You have expressed a general view.

**Thiru J. James:** It was said that a lathi charge was made because people from the procession deviated from the route. Did those at the front of the procession change course, or did those at the rear do so? Please clarify this.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** A procession means the entire procession. I need not tell the honourable member that there cannot be one rule for its front and another for its rear. That is something he can understand for himself.

---NOTES---
- “செய்திக் கதிர்” is rendered as “newsreel,” referring to the Government-produced filmed news programme discussed in the estimates.
- “ரெகுலேட்டரி ஆர்டர்” is rendered as “regulatory order,” preserving the administrative expression used in the speech.
- “டி.சி-க்கள்” is expanded contextually as “Deputy Commissioners”; the Tamil source retains the abbreviation.
- “கான்ஃப்லிக்ட்” is translated as “conflict,” reflecting the member's English loanword for contradictory police statements.
